WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.903 --> 00:00:06.320
STEWART REICHLING:
Hey, everybody.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.690
My name is Stewart
Reichling from Google Cloud.

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.420
I'm a product manager
working on Traffic Director.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.010
With me is Kelsey Hightower.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:14.750
He's going to be
doing a demo later.

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:16.490
Today we're here to
talk about building

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:21.030
an enterprise-grade service
mesh with Traffic Director.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:24.210
So I'm going to go through
a little bit of pretext

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.730
and prelude for why a
product like Traffic Director

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:29.220
even exists and why
you should consider it.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.553
Then we're going to talk about
what Traffic Director is.

00:00:31.553 --> 00:00:33.428
We're going to go into
an awesome demo that's

00:00:33.428 --> 00:00:35.160
going to give you a
good sense of how do

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.940
you interact with this product?

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.365
How does it actually work?

00:00:38.365 --> 00:00:39.990
We'll talk through
some of the problems

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:42.070
that customers are solving
with Traffic Director,

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:44.070
and then we'll go into
some of the announcements

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:47.350
that we're going to make today.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.805
So what is Traffic Director?

00:00:49.805 --> 00:00:51.180
To understand
Traffic Director, I

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.670
think it helps to kind of think
through a very simple model.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.270
So I talk to
customers all the time

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.880
who are dealing with
problems like a retailer

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.090
who wants to try to make their
deployment production ready.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:08.250
And so as a retailer, you might
be doing very retail things.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.125
You have a
shopping-cart service.

00:01:10.125 --> 00:01:11.250
You have a payment service.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:15.960
The shopping-cart service
helps your customers add items

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.790
to their carts, and
then a payment service

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.710
allows your customers
to pay for the stuff

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:20.910
that they want to buy.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.560
Really straightforward
stuff, right,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:23.977
the types of things
that you might

00:01:23.977 --> 00:01:26.483
want to solve as a retailer.

00:01:26.483 --> 00:01:27.900
Let's look at what
that looks like

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.280
once you start trying to
make it production ready.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.080
And so here I have some code.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:35.080
It's not great.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.080
It's the type of
code I might write.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.620
It's a checkout function,
a charge_customer function,

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.635
and then a function that
calls on both of those, very

00:01:43.635 --> 00:01:45.510
straightforward, the
types of things that you

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:47.700
might want to do as a retailer.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.150
And you'll notice here that
there is this line of code that

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:52.980
is the payment endpoint.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.680
This is the endpoint that
the shopping cart uses

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.550
when it wants to call
the payment service,

00:01:58.550 --> 00:01:59.940
and it's pretty straightforward.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:01.830
Customers aren't really
doing this stuff anymore,

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:02.950
but kind of bear with me.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.230
It's an IP address that
the shopping cart's

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:08.020
going to call on.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:10.289
And you'll notice that
this simple model, you've

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:12.120
got some business logic.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.650
You've got some
nonbusiness logic,

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:17.820
which is that payments endpoint,
pretty straightforward,

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.620
and small business logic.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:20.400
Not too bad.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.350
You're just calling this
thing from your shopping cart.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:27.990
And now you get to a
production scenario

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:29.990
where you need to make
this thing something that

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:31.333
works for the holiday season.

00:02:31.333 --> 00:02:32.750
And during the
holiday season, you

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:35.030
might have a lot of customers,
and you can't really

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:38.493
afford to have your payments
back end be overloaded,

00:02:38.493 --> 00:02:39.410
and so what do you do?

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:40.820
You need more capacity.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.590
Well, one way you
get more capacity

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.617
is you create another
payments back end,

00:02:45.617 --> 00:02:47.200
and your shopping
cart now needs to be

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:48.858
able to call both
of those back ends.

00:02:48.858 --> 00:02:50.900
So if one of them is
overloaded, well, that's OK.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.400
You've got another one.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.090
You can't afford to have your
payments failing because that's

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:56.777
money left on the table.

00:02:56.777 --> 00:02:59.110
And so you have two instances
of your payments back end.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:01.240
Your shopping cart needs
to kind of figure out

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.540
how to send traffic
between them.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:07.186
We'll go back to our code, and
here's the exact same snippet

00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:08.140
I showed before.

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:09.520
I hid some of the checkout
stuff because you're

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.812
going to see this starts to
become pretty clunky pretty

00:03:11.812 --> 00:03:12.490
fast.

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:16.107
So rather than calling
the one payments endpoint,

00:03:16.107 --> 00:03:18.190
we need to be able to call
two payments endpoints.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:20.977
In fact, we need to be able
to load balance between them.

00:03:20.977 --> 00:03:22.810
And so one way that you
might do this is you

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:25.930
might write a load-balanced
endpoints function.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:28.160
And so rather than calling
just that one endpoint,

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:32.830
10.0.0.5, you now have
this 10.0.0.6 as well,

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:34.700
and you randomly choose
between one of them.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:36.490
This is a pretty
straightforward,

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:39.640
not-too-complicated way of
implementing round-robin load

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:40.300
balancing.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.760
Occasionally you'll send
traffic to one endpoint.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.657
On other occasions, you'll
send traffic to the other one.

00:03:45.657 --> 00:03:47.740
And so this gives you a
pretty straightforward way

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:49.810
to just load balance
between both of those

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:51.280
payments back ends.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.500
But one of the things
that you'll notice here

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:57.040
is that whereas before I only
had that one endpoint, now

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:58.000
I have two endpoints.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.950
I've also had to think
about how I choose

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:01.870
between those endpoints,
how I load balance

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:03.190
between those endpoints.

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:05.440
And take a second to
pause and put yourself

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:07.460
in the shoes of a retailer.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:10.510
What does this stuff have to do
with selling goods and services

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:12.310
and products to your customers?

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:15.100
Well, not very much, but
you're now responsible also

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:17.470
for figuring this code out
and owning and maintaining

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:19.519
this code.

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:20.490
Pretty straightforward.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:23.490
We just figured out how we could
add another payments back end

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:24.740
and load balance between them.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.450
But you'll also
notice that if you

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:28.280
look at your
shopping-cart code, you're

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:31.610
now spending more of your time
on this nonbusiness logic.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.680
And what I mean by
nonbusiness logic here

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:35.912
is basically code
that doesn't have

00:04:35.912 --> 00:04:37.370
very much to do
with you delivering

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:38.730
your business at all.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:42.230
It has to do with things
like resiliency and uptime

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.350
and dealing with big
problems that you

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:47.180
might face that are
more infrastructure

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:50.970
level than actually delivering
goods to your customer.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:53.630
So OK, you just got a
little bit more capacity

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:54.630
for your holiday season.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:57.320
Well, now we have
a new requirement,

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:59.848
and that's we can't just
randomly choose an endpoint.

00:04:59.848 --> 00:05:01.640
We want to make sure
that the shopping cart

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.790
only calls on payments
back ends that are healthy.

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:07.250
And so rather than
just randomly choosing

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:09.830
one, which is what
we did here, is

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:11.960
we are going to actually
test those endpoints,

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:14.600
make sure that they actually are
healthy before we send traffic

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:15.310
to them.

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:17.930
And so rather than
get_load_balanced_endpoint,

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:20.510
we update that function
to be get healthy load

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:21.950
balanced endpoint.

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:24.830
And you'll notice there's even a
function here is_healthy, which

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:26.360
I don't know how to write this.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:28.620
Basically what this would
do is figure out-- it's

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.490
got that IP address, 10.0.0.5.

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:33.440
Is it actually healthy?

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:34.790
It would go and check.

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:38.160
get_healthy_endpoints would look
at, for each of the endpoints,

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:39.860
whether they are healthy or not.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:42.620
And suddenly that chunk
of code that you're owning

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:46.220
grows more and more towards
infrastructure-level problems,

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.800
networking problems, and
that's where you're dedicating

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.550
more and more of your time.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.730
And so again, like we saw
before, your code that

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:57.290
is nonbusiness logic
continues to grow.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:59.110
And again, I need
to ask you, what

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:00.880
does that have to do
with selling goods

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.740
and services to your customers?

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.030
Well, not very much.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:07.840
And the examples I just showed
you are pretty straightforward,

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.520
ones that a lot of
customers might face.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:12.850
Well, I talk to customers who
ask for all kinds of things

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:15.730
that I would put in the
nonbusiness-logic category.

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:19.060
My customers have requirements
like I have payments back

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:22.160
ends or other back ends in
multiple regions and clusters.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:25.370
I need to be able to
gracefully handle timeouts.

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:27.960
I may be handling
private information.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.800
That needs to be encrypted.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:32.860
Tracing and logging code
needs to be in there too.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.542
Dynamically scaling up
different payments back ends.

00:06:35.542 --> 00:06:37.000
I might even have
development teams

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:39.710
that want to operate in
multiple different languages.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:42.610
And so all of these
things I would posit

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:46.150
are nonbusiness logic, and
your engineering efforts

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:50.350
go to more and more handling
infrastructure concerns, which

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:54.730
takes away focus and resources
from delivering your payments,

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:56.410
from delivering
your shopping cart,

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:59.760
from delivering your
actual business.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:01.270
And so the good
news is the example

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:03.820
I just showed you is a
little bit contrived.

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:05.500
We've already found
ways to solve this.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:07.570
Customers have for a long
time been doing things

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:10.360
like running a load balancer
in between the shopping

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:12.410
cart and the payment service.

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:15.670
And so here, that load
balancer is basically a proxy.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:17.530
The shopping cart
sends traffic to it.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:21.440
The load balancer figures out
where that traffic should go.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:25.750
All of your nonbusiness logic
lives in that load balancer.

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:27.660
But even this
pattern has its flaws

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:30.940
which is that load
balancer sits in between,

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:33.780
but you might have more and
more client instances sending

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.690
traffic through it, and that
starts to become a bottleneck.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:39.547
You now have to figure out,
how do I scale this thing up?

00:07:39.547 --> 00:07:41.130
How do I make sure
it doesn't go down?

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:43.300
because it's a single
point of failure.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:46.440
And you even see other patterns
emerge that I would also

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:48.990
characterize as smells.

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:51.150
You might have, for
example, a support service.

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:53.432
Which support's important,
don't get me wrong,

00:07:53.432 --> 00:07:55.140
but at the end of the
day, your customers

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.600
are paying for
goods and services.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:58.950
They're not paying for support.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:01.740
And so that's maybe a
little bit less critical,

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.170
but I see plenty of customers
who are sending traffic

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:06.960
from both shopping-cart
critical services

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.260
and support-front-end
less critical services

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:12.060
through the same proxy.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.358
And again, you've got those
issues around scaling,

00:08:15.358 --> 00:08:17.400
making sure it doesn't go
down, but then also you

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.230
have this configuration
that now lives

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:21.600
in your proxy belonging to
multiple different types

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:22.445
of services.

00:08:22.445 --> 00:08:23.820
You need to make
sure that if you

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:26.670
make a change to the
support-front-end proxy code

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:29.760
it doesn't cause an issue
for your other services,

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:32.447
and so now you're dealing
with isolation problems.

00:08:32.447 --> 00:08:34.530
And again, all of these
things go back to the idea

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:36.480
that you aren't really
delivering value

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:38.669
to your customers
when you're working

00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:41.200
through these types of problems.

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:42.700
Over the past couple
of years, we've

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:45.790
seen a pattern that
helps us to solve this,

00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:48.880
and the idea here is you have
a dedicated client-side load

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:50.260
balancer.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:52.570
Often that is a sidecar
proxy, and so that

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:54.940
might run right next
to your shopping cart.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:57.790
On the left-hand side, you
see an individual instance

00:08:57.790 --> 00:08:59.260
of that shopping cart.

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:01.210
It has its own
sidecar proxy that

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:04.840
only has logic for the
shopping-cart routing,

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:07.330
for load balancing
for the shopping cart.

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:09.520
And if you want to
scale up and you

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:11.500
have another
shopping-cart instance,

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:13.990
well, that has its
own sidecar proxy.

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:15.970
And so both of those
can independently

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:19.090
load balance between
your payments back ends.

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:22.210
If one shopping-cart instance
or one shopping-cart instance's

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:26.290
sidecar proxy misbehaves,
the other one is unaffected.

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:28.720
And so this gives you a
really nice, simple way

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:31.100
to have client-side
load balancing.

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:33.820
You get rid of those concerns
around bottlenecks of things

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:35.050
that sit in the middle.

00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:38.020
You've got configuration
that's highly tailored just

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:38.940
to that shopping cart.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:41.770
It's not shared across
multiple services.

00:09:41.770 --> 00:09:43.390
That's what the
sidecar-proxy model

00:09:43.390 --> 00:09:47.010
that we've seen in the last
couple of years can do for you.

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:48.930
And so far, so good.

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:51.480
You've basically solved a lot
of the problems I described

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:54.810
earlier, but now you're getting
yourself into a new game,

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:56.580
and that is that you
need to figure out

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:59.328
how you're going to
configure that sidecar proxy.

00:09:59.328 --> 00:10:01.620
So one way you might do that--
and I just made this up.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:03.900
This is a proxy.conf file.

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:07.810
Imagine a JSON file
or a YAML file.

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:10.710
It contains the configuration
for your sidecar.

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:14.190
It has the endpoints for
the payments back ends.

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:18.330
It has maybe how you're going
to do health checks for those.

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:21.000
It's got the information
that the proxy needs in order

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:23.570
to support that shopping cart.

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:24.570
And so OK, fine.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:28.710
Writing a proxy.conf for one
sidecar proxy, no big deal,

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:30.090
right?

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:33.210
And so what happens when you
have a new payments back end?

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:35.430
Well, you're configuring
your proxies again.

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:37.440
And so a way to do
that is you write

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:39.500
yourself another proxy.conf.

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:42.180
And I think you can already
see where I'm going with this,

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:44.190
but you've now
effectively started

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:46.620
to take on this additional
responsibility, which

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:52.970
is how do I write, distribute,
store proxy configuration?

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:56.040
And in this case, this
is still pretty trivial.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:59.640
When I talk to customers who are
doing really enterprise-grade

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:03.120
stuff, they have departments
that look far more complicated.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:04.620
They have deployments
that look more

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:08.550
like this, multiple
regions, multiple services,

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:11.400
some even hundreds or
thousands of services, each

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:13.710
running their own
sidecar proxies or middle

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:18.450
proxies with things like
encryption turned on

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:20.790
or different
load-balancing policies.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:22.920
And so now you've got
yourself into the game

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:26.250
of writing and configuring
proxy.conf files.

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:29.100
And if you talk about expanding
the number of services,

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:31.320
expanding the number of
regions, this all just

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:33.390
becomes way more complicated.

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:36.930
You see customers doing
things like checking these in,

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:40.740
have validation in place
for proxy.conf files,

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:42.930
figuring out how to
make sure that there's

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:45.445
no issues in those
proxy.conf files.

00:11:45.445 --> 00:11:47.820
And I'll go back to the thing
at the very beginning which

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:50.280
is if you are a
retailer, what does

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:52.260
that have to do
with selling goods

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:54.700
and services to your customers?

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:56.940
And again, people
have solved this.

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:58.620
There's a solution for it.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:00.090
It's called a control plane.

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:02.940
And so the control
plane is a service that

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:07.710
is basically responsible
for writing, distributing,

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:12.090
making sure proxy.conf gets
to the right sidecar proxies.

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:13.650
It might have an API.

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:16.530
It might have different checks
to make sure the sidecar

00:12:16.530 --> 00:12:18.000
proxies are helpful.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:20.490
And you've probably
seen things like this.

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:22.630
For example, Istio
is a great example.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:24.990
Istio has a component
called Pilot.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:27.540
And all that really
is a control plane

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:29.130
that runs in your cluster.

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:30.970
You have some APIs
to tell it, hey,

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:33.370
this is what the world
should look like.

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:35.760
These are the policies
that I want to enforce.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:38.550
And then Pilot will take
that and go and distribute it

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:40.840
to all of your sidecar proxies.

00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:44.920
And so I talk about Pilot as
an example of a control plane.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:47.880
Another example of a control
plane is Traffic Director.

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:51.580
Traffic Director is a
control plane as a service.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:53.630
It's a GCP-managed service.

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:56.220
I'll talk a little
bit more about what

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:58.210
makes Traffic Director special.

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:00.480
But at its core,
it's a control plane.

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:03.130
You don't have to install it,
update it, any of that stuff.

00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:04.890
It runs within GCP.

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:07.530
It knows about the world
because you tell it what

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:08.680
the world should look like.

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:10.320
And it also collects
signals really

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:12.480
intelligently from
your infrastructure,

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:16.510
and it generates that
configuration for your proxies.

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:18.023
And so what is Traffic Director?

00:13:18.023 --> 00:13:20.440
Well, there's four things that
I want you to keep in mind.

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:24.090
Traffic Director is a universal
managed control plane.

00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:26.100
And so what do I mean
when I mean universal?

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:30.160
I mean one part of that is
that it's global by default.

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:33.580
It looks at your whole
network as a global thing,

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:36.660
and so that includes not
just containers but also

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:38.580
virtual machines.

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:43.110
Plenty of customers aren't ready
to go 100% into Kubernetes yet,

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:45.090
and that's totally OK.

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:47.490
Whether you're on
VMs, on containers,

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:50.010
Traffic Director really
doesn't make a distinction

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:52.650
about which services
it's supporting.

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:55.380
If it's got, for
example, an Envoy

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:58.560
proxy on the virtual machine
or on the container that

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:02.040
connects to Traffic Director
using a set of APIs known

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:04.320
as the XDS APIs,
Traffic Director

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.275
will send a configuration back.

00:14:06.275 --> 00:14:08.400
And the other thing that's
really cool that Traffic

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:11.580
Director is it's not just GCP.

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:13.640
We're starting to now have--

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:16.300
and you'll see more
about this later.

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:18.480
Customers have
deployments in GCP

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:20.610
and non-GCP environments
and other clouds.

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:22.350
And so for us, being
able to support

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:25.140
those other environments
is really key.

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:28.043
You'll see in a second about
what that means practically

00:14:28.043 --> 00:14:29.460
in terms of what
we're delivering,

00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:31.620
but just think of Traffic
Director as global,

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:33.590
as something bigger
than just GCP,

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:35.982
as something that's
bigger than just VMs.

00:14:35.982 --> 00:14:37.440
So if I can reinforce
that further,

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:39.360
it's the fact that
Traffic Director is

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:41.760
more than just a service mesh.

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:44.400
Service mesh is a
type of deployment

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:46.440
that Traffic
Director can support.

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:48.980
It works really well
with service mesh.

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:52.020
But we're also starting
to see customers working

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:53.790
with more than
just these sidecar

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:55.950
proxies deployed in
the service mesh.

00:14:55.950 --> 00:14:58.530
We're starting to see customers
who are using application

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:00.600
libraries without
proxies, and we'll

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:03.630
talk about what that
means in a second as well.

00:15:03.630 --> 00:15:06.660
And also we're seeing customers
who are just deploying an Envoy

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:09.480
proxy on a virtual machine
that acts as a load

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:12.790
balancer, similar to that
model I showed earlier,

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:15.090
the one before the
sidecar-based model,

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:18.600
where you don't necessarily
have to be ready to add sidecar

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:22.050
proxies everywhere to get
value from Traffic Director.

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:24.000
Our goal is to support
a lot more than just

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:26.100
the traditional
model where you have

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:28.770
sidecar proxies next to
your application workloads.

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:30.810
We want to follow you
where your deployment is,

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:32.727
and what your topology
looks like is something

00:15:32.727 --> 00:15:35.280
that we want to support.

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:37.800
The third thing to keep in
mind is that's programmable.

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:41.430
So we've got API that you use
to configure Traffic Director.

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:44.260
We've got a rich
set of policies--

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:47.898
so traffic-management policies,
layer-7-based policies.

00:15:47.898 --> 00:15:50.190
We've got a lot of other
interesting policies coming up

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:51.300
as well.

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:52.927
But this gives you
a centralized way

00:15:52.927 --> 00:15:54.385
to manage all of
your configuration

00:15:54.385 --> 00:15:57.180
and your policies
in a single place.

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:00.120
You don't need to worry
about distributing policies,

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:03.340
about sort of handcrafting them.

00:16:03.340 --> 00:16:05.160
It's a programmable
interface that you

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:07.862
can use to configure
your application network.

00:16:07.862 --> 00:16:10.320
And then finally-- and this is
what really distinguishes it

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:11.737
from a lot of other
solutions-- is

00:16:11.737 --> 00:16:13.860
it's a control
plane as a service.

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:17.880
What I mean here is that it's a
managed service managed by GCP.

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:18.930
It's got an SLA.

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:22.000
Our SREs are kind of making
sure that it doesn't go down,

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:24.780
and if there is an issue, we're
taking care of that for you.

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:26.280
It gets rid of a
lot of the things

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:30.120
that I would call not really
related to your business around

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:31.590
how do I install
a control plane?

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:32.500
How do I keep it up?

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:34.920
How do I make sure that the
state of my control plane

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:37.540
is replicated across
multiple regions?

00:16:37.540 --> 00:16:39.540
These are all really
fundamentally difficult

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:42.880
problems that if you're a
retailer, if you're a bank,

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:45.300
if you're a
pharmaceutical company,

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:47.550
that's not really your
business, and you're not really

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:52.127
deriving much value from
taking on that responsibility.

00:16:52.127 --> 00:16:53.710
And so with that,
I'm going to pass it

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:56.060
on to Kelsey who's
going to make it real.

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:57.360
He's going to show a demo.

00:16:57.360 --> 00:16:59.880
Kelsey, off to you.

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:01.880
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: All
right, thank you, Stewart.

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:04.032
So we're going to
jump right into it.

00:17:04.032 --> 00:17:05.740
As we just talked
about, Traffic Director

00:17:05.740 --> 00:17:08.930
is kind of center for all
of our service configuration

00:17:08.930 --> 00:17:11.869
and the heart of our
service mesh in GCP.

00:17:11.869 --> 00:17:14.319
It's often paired with
a thing called Envoy.

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:17.300
So sidecar, some people
look at it as just a proxy.

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:20.300
The nice thing about it is
Envoy supports the standard xDS

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:22.130
protocol for its configuration.

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:24.470
That means I can point
it at Traffic Director

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:26.563
to grab all this configuration.

00:17:26.563 --> 00:17:28.730
A lot of you will be familiar
with a similar process

00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:30.890
that you see in
something like Istio,

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:34.970
but what happens if the overhead
of having an additional sidecar

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:37.280
like Envoy becomes
too high, maybe

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:39.030
for a high-performance scenario?

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:40.670
Or what about the
management overhead

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:43.997
of having to manage another
binary and its configuration?

00:17:43.997 --> 00:17:46.580
And the interesting thing that
we're going to talk about today

00:17:46.580 --> 00:17:49.022
is proxyless service mesh.

00:17:49.022 --> 00:17:50.480
If you think about
it, there's lots

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:52.450
of high-performance
frameworks out there.

00:17:52.450 --> 00:17:54.640
GRPC is one of my favorites.

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:58.830
Inside of gRPC, we now have
native xDS integration.

00:17:58.830 --> 00:17:59.792
So what does that mean?

00:17:59.792 --> 00:18:01.250
Well, it means that
we can actually

00:18:01.250 --> 00:18:04.340
get our configuration
from Traffic Director just

00:18:04.340 --> 00:18:05.480
like Envoy.

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:07.080
And once we have
our configuration,

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:08.780
we can actually have
back ends, and we

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:10.700
can do some of the
traffic-management features

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:14.810
that you normally find in a
broader service mesh or a proxy

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:16.310
like Envoy.

00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:19.740
Now to set this up,
we need some services.

00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:21.800
Now, I can run my
services on VMs,

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:24.983
but Kubernetes is one of
my favorite platforms.

00:18:24.983 --> 00:18:27.650
So what we're looking at here is
just a basic Kubernetes cluster

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:28.940
called next.

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:31.670
If I were to drill into
this Kubernetes cluster,

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:33.998
I have a deployment
of my calculator app,

00:18:33.998 --> 00:18:35.540
and I'll show you
how that calculator

00:18:35.540 --> 00:18:37.040
app works in a moment.

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:39.440
Just know I have a
set of containers

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:41.475
deployed to Kubernetes.

00:18:41.475 --> 00:18:43.100
Now, I have three of
these, and they're

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:47.610
set up with an auto scalar
to go up to 10 if I need it.

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:49.580
Now typically when you
have multiple instances

00:18:49.580 --> 00:18:52.190
of something, you'll typically
have a load balancer, so

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:55.120
let's click into that.

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:57.540
So here is my service
configuration.

00:18:57.540 --> 00:19:01.620
You'll notice this is just a
standard Kubernetes Service

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:02.190
object.

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:04.725
There is no load
balancer at all.

00:19:04.725 --> 00:19:08.692
All I have is a collection of
pods and their IP addresses.

00:19:08.692 --> 00:19:10.650
So we're going to need
something else to handle

00:19:10.650 --> 00:19:12.990
the load-balancing
aspects for us.

00:19:12.990 --> 00:19:17.260
Now the nice thing about this is
the proxyless gRPC integration,

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:19.410
we can actually do
this on the client side

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:22.540
by getting the configuration
from Traffic Director.

00:19:22.540 --> 00:19:24.960
So before we do that,
we need to make sure

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:28.480
we understand how the
calculator app actually works.

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:30.360
So the thing you
have to keep in mind

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:33.270
is we're not going to change
the way we write code here.

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:36.210
For example, I can write a
standard gRPC application

00:19:36.210 --> 00:19:38.980
and test it on my laptop,
so let's do that now.

00:19:38.980 --> 00:19:41.500
I'm just going to cd into
this calculator directory.

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:43.110
This is where the
source code lives.

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:44.940
And here's the server instance.

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:47.020
I'll just go ahead and
build that really quickly

00:19:47.020 --> 00:19:49.050
so I can actually take
a detailed look at how

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:51.000
it actually works.

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:54.540
Now, the server calculator
app, all it does is builds

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:57.060
an endpoint where I
can call an add method

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:59.600
and give it an array of numbers
and have it give us back

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:02.400
a result. Once that's
done compiling,

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:04.860
we'll run it right
here from my laptop.

00:20:04.860 --> 00:20:06.030
So here we go.

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:07.740
We'll run that here.

00:20:07.740 --> 00:20:09.250
So you can see
that it's starting,

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:13.910
listening on port 5051,
commonly the default gRPC port.

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:16.590
And I also have my health
checks on port 8080.

00:20:16.590 --> 00:20:18.970
So that's running locally here.

00:20:18.970 --> 00:20:21.520
The next thing we'll do is
we'll take a look at the client.

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:24.010
Now the client-side code
is pretty straightforward.

00:20:24.010 --> 00:20:25.763
I'll cd into that directory.

00:20:25.763 --> 00:20:27.180
And then what we'll
do here is I'm

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:30.310
going to compile the client.

00:20:30.310 --> 00:20:32.640
And this is going to
be a very standard kind

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:35.940
of request-and-response
gRPC application.

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:38.320
So as we're building our
client, once it's set up,

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:40.530
we'll take a look at
its command-line flags

00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:43.918
to see what options we have.

00:20:43.918 --> 00:20:45.710
All right, so now that
the client is built,

00:20:45.710 --> 00:20:48.900
let's take a look
at those flags.

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:51.210
And we'll see that we can
give it a calculator flag,

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:53.970
and we can tell it where the
calculator service is running.

00:20:53.970 --> 00:20:55.710
So by default,
it's going to look

00:20:55.710 --> 00:21:00.720
on service host on port
50051, and then we can give it

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:02.130
a list of things to calculate.

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:04.090
Pretty straightforward.

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:05.410
So let's just run that now.

00:21:05.410 --> 00:21:06.923
So we'll say client,
and then we'll

00:21:06.923 --> 00:21:08.090
give it a couple of numbers.

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:10.477
We'll say 10, 20.

00:21:10.477 --> 00:21:12.060
And you don't have
to be great at math

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:13.810
to get the answer for
this, and we can see

00:21:13.810 --> 00:21:16.110
that the result is 30, right?

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:17.890
Pretty straightforward.

00:21:17.890 --> 00:21:21.690
Now, what if we want to send
our application the server

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:22.770
somewhere else?

00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:25.830
Well, remember, I packaged
up that server component

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:28.350
into this calculator app
that's running inside

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:31.380
of GKE or Kubernetes,
and you'll see here

00:21:31.380 --> 00:21:33.420
that we have multiple
instances running.

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:36.100
Ideally, you want a HA service.

00:21:36.100 --> 00:21:38.200
And then we also have
it configured to listen

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:42.920
onto this port, 50051.

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:45.020
Now, what if I
wanted to actually do

00:21:45.020 --> 00:21:47.563
this from a virtual machine
and no longer on my laptop?

00:21:47.563 --> 00:21:49.730
Now we have to deal with
the complexities of service

00:21:49.730 --> 00:21:51.980
discovery and making sure
that we can load ballots

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:54.025
across all of those back ends.

00:21:54.025 --> 00:21:55.400
So the first step
we've got to do

00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:57.200
before we can do
anything else, we need

00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:00.115
to configure Traffic Director.

00:22:00.115 --> 00:22:01.990
So I'm going to pop over
to Traffic Director.

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:06.360
Now what you'll see here is
that I have one service healthy.

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:07.960
But if I scroll
down here, you'll

00:22:07.960 --> 00:22:10.590
actually see
something interesting.

00:22:10.590 --> 00:22:13.470
You'll see that I have one
network endpoint group.

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:16.200
When I created my service
instead of Kubernetes,

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:19.140
I told the GKE cluster
to actually manage

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:23.020
a network endpoint group that
has all those pods behind it.

00:22:23.020 --> 00:22:25.440
So if we have 3
or 10, all of them

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:28.170
will be grouped behind this
network endpoint group.

00:22:28.170 --> 00:22:30.360
You also see this
green checkbox here.

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:32.580
What this is saying is
that Traffic Director

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:34.140
is doing its own
set of health checks

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:36.240
to make sure that
all of those pods

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:38.130
are actually healthy
before we send

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:40.200
the configuration with
those back ends in it

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:43.840
to any client-side gRPC
application looking to leverage

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:45.000
these.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:48.480
The last thing we need here
is also how do we communicate

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:50.400
what service do we want?

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:53.280
And this is where some of the
routing rules come into play.

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:56.070
Now if you have any experience
with Google Cloud load-balancer

00:22:56.070 --> 00:22:59.130
products, this is going to
look very familiar to you.

00:22:59.130 --> 00:23:02.160
First, we're going to start
with the gRPC protocol.

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:03.960
We're going to associate
a set of services

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:06.560
that we created early
in the Services tab.

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:10.080
And if you click on calculator
here, what we'll see

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:13.170
is that we give it a
couple of name matches.

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:15.810
So here, calculator plus
that port combination

00:23:15.810 --> 00:23:20.050
will route us to this gRPC
service called calculator,

00:23:20.050 --> 00:23:20.550
right?

00:23:20.550 --> 00:23:24.390
So now all the configuration
is set on the server side.

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:27.180
Now the next thing we have
to do on the client side

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:30.720
is we need to tell
gRPC how to do this.

00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:32.730
Now you're probably
thinking, do I

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:35.980
have to rewrite my entire
application to make this work?

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:38.550
Well, the good news is
with one import statement,

00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:40.208
we can make this
work automatically.

00:23:40.208 --> 00:23:42.000
I'm just going to pop
over to GitHub really

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:44.703
quickly so we can actually
see what the code looks like.

00:23:44.703 --> 00:23:46.620
So here we're going to
click on our main code.

00:23:46.620 --> 00:23:48.203
I'm just going to
zoom in a little bit

00:23:48.203 --> 00:23:50.110
here so we can see
what's going on.

00:23:50.110 --> 00:23:53.190
This is the only import
that we have to do.

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:54.690
And if you look
at this, we're not

00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:56.220
going to actually
use xDS anywhere.

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:58.710
We're just going to load
the client-side load

00:23:58.710 --> 00:24:00.920
balancing and service-discovery
components that

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:03.790
make it compatible
with Traffic Director.

00:24:03.790 --> 00:24:08.540
So this is an xDS-compliant
client-side load balancing.

00:24:08.540 --> 00:24:11.040
So with that in place, we've
got one more step that we need.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:14.500
We actually have to
tell that bit of code

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:16.910
where Traffic Director is.

00:24:16.910 --> 00:24:19.000
So here we have a service URI.

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:21.350
So this is going to be
our xDS control plane,

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:23.500
in this case, Traffic Director.

00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:25.690
We're going to use our
server's account credentials

00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:27.648
so we can authenticate to it.

00:24:27.648 --> 00:24:29.440
And when we register
with Traffic Director,

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:32.290
we have to pass a little bit
of information about ourselves,

00:24:32.290 --> 00:24:37.000
mainly our project number and
what network we care about.

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:38.470
Now, one thing to
keep in mind here

00:24:38.470 --> 00:24:40.120
is my Kubernetes
cluster is running

00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:43.600
in a network in my VPC called
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:45.370
And that's super
important, and we

00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:47.890
had to let Traffic Director
know that's the network that we

00:24:47.890 --> 00:24:49.880
would like to find services in.

00:24:49.880 --> 00:24:51.700
And the last thing
is Traffic Director's

00:24:51.700 --> 00:24:56.140
really intelligent about routing
our traffic to, let's say,

00:24:56.140 --> 00:24:57.410
the nearest zone.

00:24:57.410 --> 00:25:01.480
So if I'm in US 1-a and I have
multiple Kubernetes clusters

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:03.637
with the same
application deployed,

00:25:03.637 --> 00:25:05.470
it will be nice to have
Traffic Director use

00:25:05.470 --> 00:25:08.170
our locality
information to give me

00:25:08.170 --> 00:25:11.590
an optimized set of back ends,
maybe for things like latency

00:25:11.590 --> 00:25:13.720
or, if a zone is
down, redirected me

00:25:13.720 --> 00:25:16.600
to another zone
that's available.

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:19.090
All right, so now that we have
all the configuration, well,

00:25:19.090 --> 00:25:23.055
let's copy this
client binary to a VM.

00:25:23.055 --> 00:25:24.430
So what we're
going to do here is

00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:27.700
I'm just going to copy
the prebuilt binary to one

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:30.753
of our VMs that I have
in my infrastructure.

00:25:30.753 --> 00:25:32.920
So we'll just take a look
at that calculator binary.

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:34.628
Make sure that we have
it for the client.

00:25:34.628 --> 00:25:35.355
You see it here.

00:25:35.355 --> 00:25:36.980
And what I'm going
to do really quickly

00:25:36.980 --> 00:25:41.500
is I'm just going to scp
this binary to a little VM I

00:25:41.500 --> 00:25:42.763
call proxyless-grpc.

00:25:42.763 --> 00:25:44.680
So let's just run that
command really quickly.

00:25:48.545 --> 00:25:49.920
So what we're
going to do here is

00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:51.920
we're just going to copy
this binary that I just

00:25:51.920 --> 00:25:56.710
ran on my laptop, unmodified
code, to that virtual machine.

00:25:56.710 --> 00:26:02.010
And now what we're going to do
is SSH to that virtual machine.

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:03.680
And once we're on
that virtual machine,

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:06.320
we'll have the ability to
run the same code again,

00:26:06.320 --> 00:26:10.370
but this time we're going to try
to hit the containers running

00:26:10.370 --> 00:26:12.240
in Kubernetes.

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:13.955
So we need a couple
of things first.

00:26:13.955 --> 00:26:16.080
We need to make sure that
our environment is set up

00:26:16.080 --> 00:26:17.200
correctly.

00:26:17.200 --> 00:26:20.070
We have to tell xDS where
our bootstrap file is.

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:22.080
So you'll see this
environment variable

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:24.300
being set to my
local file system

00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:28.257
where the xDS bootstrap
configuration file is.

00:26:28.257 --> 00:26:29.840
So we'll just source
that to make sure

00:26:29.840 --> 00:26:32.000
that our environment is ready.

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:34.700
And this time, we're going to
do something slightly different

00:26:34.700 --> 00:26:36.067
with our client connection.

00:26:36.067 --> 00:26:37.900
So we're not dealing
with localhost anymore.

00:26:37.900 --> 00:26:40.130
We'll do something
slightly different.

00:26:40.130 --> 00:26:41.990
With our calculator
flag, we're going

00:26:41.990 --> 00:26:45.230
to give it the xDS schema,
and what this will do

00:26:45.230 --> 00:26:48.710
is it will tell the xDS library
and all our client-side load

00:26:48.710 --> 00:26:53.480
balancing to look up the service
inside of Traffic Director.

00:26:53.480 --> 00:26:54.980
That's going to be
just enough so it

00:26:54.980 --> 00:26:57.500
bypasses the normal lookup
service, in my case,

00:26:57.500 --> 00:26:59.088
DNS by default.

00:26:59.088 --> 00:27:00.380
Let's give it a couple numbers.

00:27:00.380 --> 00:27:02.170
We have 1 and 56.

00:27:02.170 --> 00:27:04.070
We'll run it now.

00:27:04.070 --> 00:27:06.860
And we see that we
get the results of 57.

00:27:06.860 --> 00:27:08.910
Just to make sure that
this is a live demo,

00:27:08.910 --> 00:27:10.910
let's just pick some
random numbers here.

00:27:10.910 --> 00:27:14.420
1 plus 999 is 1,000.

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:15.620
Everything is working great.

00:27:15.620 --> 00:27:17.600
And I know you
might be thinking,

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:18.800
this is a virtual machine.

00:27:18.800 --> 00:27:22.040
How hard would it be to install
Envoy in the virtual machine

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:24.580
and just use Envoy to
keep everything standard?

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:26.400
And you'll make a good point.

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:29.700
It doesn't necessarily address
the need for high performance

00:27:29.700 --> 00:27:32.300
if you want to avoid the
proxy, but you're right.

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:34.940
On a virtual machine,
we do have the ability

00:27:34.940 --> 00:27:37.420
to just run Envoy as
a sidecar right there

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:40.880
on the virtual machine and
bypass doing any of this.

00:27:40.880 --> 00:27:43.177
So there's another thing
that I wanted to try.

00:27:43.177 --> 00:27:45.260
Now, this might get me in
trouble with the PM team

00:27:45.260 --> 00:27:48.290
because this is not something
that we advertise as working,

00:27:48.290 --> 00:27:51.860
but what's a live demo without
a little bit of creativity?

00:27:51.860 --> 00:27:53.570
One environment
that I like a lot

00:27:53.570 --> 00:27:56.750
and really makes sense for
a proxyless integration

00:27:56.750 --> 00:27:59.877
with Traffic Director
is Cloud Run.

00:27:59.877 --> 00:28:01.460
If you're not familiar
with Cloud Run,

00:28:01.460 --> 00:28:05.540
it's our serverless offering
where I can run my containers.

00:28:05.540 --> 00:28:08.080
So earlier we just showed
running on a laptop,

00:28:08.080 --> 00:28:11.240
running on a virtual machine,
but think about Cloud Run.

00:28:11.240 --> 00:28:13.010
I don't have the
option of running

00:28:13.010 --> 00:28:14.780
any additional sidecars.

00:28:14.780 --> 00:28:18.120
I can only provide my
application, and that's it.

00:28:18.120 --> 00:28:19.580
So in this particular
environment,

00:28:19.580 --> 00:28:22.490
it's pretty constrained
in terms of what I can do.

00:28:22.490 --> 00:28:25.640
So the nice thing about this
is I have the ability now

00:28:25.640 --> 00:28:27.710
to do the exact
same thing that I

00:28:27.710 --> 00:28:29.540
was doing on that
virtual machine

00:28:29.540 --> 00:28:32.510
on a serverless
offering like Cloud Run.

00:28:32.510 --> 00:28:34.520
You'll even notice
here that I'm passing

00:28:34.520 --> 00:28:37.610
in the command-line flag that
matches the exact same one

00:28:37.610 --> 00:28:38.960
that I was running earlier.

00:28:38.960 --> 00:28:41.043
Now, we did something a
little bit different here.

00:28:41.043 --> 00:28:43.500
I took all of that client
code that you saw earlier,

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:45.290
and I moved it into
an HTTP handler.

00:28:45.290 --> 00:28:47.750
So if this is all
working, I should

00:28:47.750 --> 00:28:52.130
be able to call curl,
hit the Cloud Run URL,

00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:54.500
and then the client
code will basically

00:28:54.500 --> 00:28:58.145
use the same address that
we were using before,

00:28:58.145 --> 00:29:01.880
call out to Traffic Director,
get all of the back ends

00:29:01.880 --> 00:29:03.410
that it needs to
route traffic to.

00:29:03.410 --> 00:29:05.790
Now, there's one
more gotcha here.

00:29:05.790 --> 00:29:09.830
Cloud Run runs in a slightly
different network than my VPC,

00:29:09.830 --> 00:29:12.320
so I had to set
up one more thing.

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:14.270
Before I could run
my application,

00:29:14.270 --> 00:29:17.990
I had to set up a VPC connector.

00:29:17.990 --> 00:29:20.630
Now in the serverless world,
we can set up these connectors

00:29:20.630 --> 00:29:23.730
to be very specific
to a specific network.

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:25.970
In this case, the Google
Kubernetes network

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:29.150
that I have set up earlier is
where my Kubernetes cluster is

00:29:29.150 --> 00:29:31.580
running and all of
those containers,

00:29:31.580 --> 00:29:33.440
and it also matches
the configuration

00:29:33.440 --> 00:29:35.300
that Traffic Director has.

00:29:35.300 --> 00:29:39.020
All I'm doing here is setting
up this serverless connector

00:29:39.020 --> 00:29:40.620
called calculator.

00:29:40.620 --> 00:29:43.110
So when I deploy my
Cloud Run application,

00:29:43.110 --> 00:29:46.400
I can also give it
that VPC connector name

00:29:46.400 --> 00:29:49.130
to ensure that when this
container starts up,

00:29:49.130 --> 00:29:53.420
it will also be able to
have a leg into that VPC.

00:29:53.420 --> 00:29:56.300
And the last thing, we can
also replicate the fact

00:29:56.300 --> 00:29:59.400
that I have this xDS
bootstrap config inside

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:00.900
of this environment
variable letting

00:30:00.900 --> 00:30:03.472
me know where to resolve,
just like in the VM.

00:30:03.472 --> 00:30:05.180
And we'll take one
more peek at something

00:30:05.180 --> 00:30:07.638
else just to make sure we're
all clear on how this actually

00:30:07.638 --> 00:30:08.360
works.

00:30:08.360 --> 00:30:10.063
If I go over here
to the calculator,

00:30:10.063 --> 00:30:11.480
I want to show you
the Docker file

00:30:11.480 --> 00:30:13.460
that I used to create
that container.

00:30:13.460 --> 00:30:15.377
I want to show you that
we don't actually have

00:30:15.377 --> 00:30:16.820
to change our code too much.

00:30:16.820 --> 00:30:19.460
There's a lot of noise here,
so I'm going to break it down.

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:21.840
I'm building the calculator
app just like I built earlier

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:22.930
on my laptop.

00:30:22.930 --> 00:30:24.620
The big difference
that I'm doing here

00:30:24.620 --> 00:30:26.990
is I'm taking the
binary from this step,

00:30:26.990 --> 00:30:30.830
and I'm also copying in
this xds-bootstrap config

00:30:30.830 --> 00:30:32.642
into the container image.

00:30:32.642 --> 00:30:34.100
Now, the nice thing
about that is I

00:30:34.100 --> 00:30:36.830
have all the things I
need necessary to call

00:30:36.830 --> 00:30:38.510
Traffic Director.

00:30:38.510 --> 00:30:41.480
And I run one deployment script,
and if everything is working,

00:30:41.480 --> 00:30:45.420
I should be able to call
that particular endpoint.

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:47.910
So I have my curl command.

00:30:47.910 --> 00:30:50.420
This is the URL from Cloud Run.

00:30:50.420 --> 00:30:53.300
And what we'll do is we'll
pass in a few integers here

00:30:53.300 --> 00:30:56.048
to see if we can get
this calculation.

00:30:56.048 --> 00:30:57.590
You can see it coming
up pretty fast.

00:30:57.590 --> 00:30:59.240
Those are pretty big
numbers, and some of you

00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:00.990
may be struggling with
math, so let's just

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:03.200
make it slightly
easier for the viewers.

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:07.300
So we'll give you
1, 3, and 5, and you

00:31:07.300 --> 00:31:10.180
see that our answer is 9.

00:31:10.180 --> 00:31:13.070
And with that, I would like
to end this presentation.

00:31:13.070 --> 00:31:14.710
As you can see,
Traffic Director is

00:31:14.710 --> 00:31:17.650
super flexible in terms of
implementing a standard xDS

00:31:17.650 --> 00:31:18.610
protocol.

00:31:18.610 --> 00:31:21.740
Combine that with
proxyless gRPC integration.

00:31:21.740 --> 00:31:25.940
We can now take a standard xDS
client, run it on my laptop,

00:31:25.940 --> 00:31:28.720
a virtual machine, or even
a serverless environment

00:31:28.720 --> 00:31:30.370
like Cloud Run,
and we don't have

00:31:30.370 --> 00:31:33.130
to match any other
binaries to get

00:31:33.130 --> 00:31:36.010
what feels like a native
service mesh integration.

00:31:36.010 --> 00:31:37.780
And with that, back
to you, Stewart.

00:31:40.252 --> 00:31:41.710
STEWART REICHLING:
All right, nice.

00:31:41.710 --> 00:31:44.250
Thank you very much, Kelsey.

00:31:44.250 --> 00:31:47.040
So far we've gone
through context

00:31:47.040 --> 00:31:50.490
on why something like Traffic
Director exists, what it is.

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:52.830
Kelsey brought it to
life by showing you

00:31:52.830 --> 00:31:54.490
how someone might
interact with it,

00:31:54.490 --> 00:31:57.250
what are some of the really
cool things you can do with it.

00:31:57.250 --> 00:31:58.830
Another thing I
wanted to get into

00:31:58.830 --> 00:32:01.950
is just what I see
customers actually

00:32:01.950 --> 00:32:04.860
using this product for because
it's good to understand it.

00:32:04.860 --> 00:32:08.430
It's good to see some hands
on, but what are real problems

00:32:08.430 --> 00:32:11.322
that customers are solving
when they use Traffic Director?

00:32:11.322 --> 00:32:12.780
And I'm going to
interweave in this

00:32:12.780 --> 00:32:16.100
some of the new announcements
that we have as well.

00:32:16.100 --> 00:32:18.740
And so let's go back to
the case of a retailer.

00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:21.970
And I know I keep harping on
these large global retailers,

00:32:21.970 --> 00:32:24.480
but I think they're a great
example because in retail, you

00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:27.250
really can't afford
any downtime.

00:32:27.250 --> 00:32:30.180
And so something like
Black Friday coming up

00:32:30.180 --> 00:32:34.600
where every single second
is money lost if you're down

00:32:34.600 --> 00:32:37.950
is exactly where a product
like Traffic Director shines.

00:32:37.950 --> 00:32:40.350
And so what you see in
front of you right now

00:32:40.350 --> 00:32:42.660
is an example of the
type of deployment

00:32:42.660 --> 00:32:44.910
that you might see
with Traffic Director.

00:32:44.910 --> 00:32:47.340
So we've got Traffic
Director running separate

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:50.290
from your regions, separate
from your deployment.

00:32:50.290 --> 00:32:54.300
It's a managed service that you
don't have to manage yourself.

00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:56.445
And here we've got an
example deployment where

00:32:56.445 --> 00:32:57.570
you're in multiple regions.

00:32:57.570 --> 00:32:58.800
You're in uscentral1.

00:32:58.800 --> 00:33:00.660
You're in asia-southeast1.

00:33:00.660 --> 00:33:03.040
You've got replicas
of the same services.

00:33:03.040 --> 00:33:07.200
So you might have a retail front
end hosted in Kubernetes Engine

00:33:07.200 --> 00:33:10.740
in both us-central1
and in asia-southeast1.

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:13.500
You have that because some
of your customers maybe

00:33:13.500 --> 00:33:14.430
you are in the US.

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:16.860
Other customers are
in asia-southeast1.

00:33:16.860 --> 00:33:20.070
So you want to make sure that
when they send you a request,

00:33:20.070 --> 00:33:23.340
it goes to the nearest services
so you reduce your latency.

00:33:23.340 --> 00:33:26.827
You reduce your
network travel costs.

00:33:26.827 --> 00:33:28.410
You also want to
make sure that you've

00:33:28.410 --> 00:33:31.475
got replicated services
in case one goes down.

00:33:31.475 --> 00:33:32.850
And so what I'm
going to show you

00:33:32.850 --> 00:33:35.250
is how Traffic Director
makes it super easy to just

00:33:35.250 --> 00:33:36.420
automatically failover.

00:33:36.420 --> 00:33:38.970
So you've got your retail
front end, your shopping

00:33:38.970 --> 00:33:40.680
cart, your payments.

00:33:40.680 --> 00:33:42.900
Each of these are hosted
in multiple regions

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:46.020
so that you can meet those
goals around latency,

00:33:46.020 --> 00:33:48.730
around costs, high availability.

00:33:48.730 --> 00:33:50.820
And so customers are
using Traffic Director

00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:54.150
in combination with other
GCP products as well.

00:33:54.150 --> 00:33:56.010
What you see here on
the left-hand side

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:57.430
is the global load balancer.

00:33:57.430 --> 00:34:01.230
So this might be a global
HTTPS load balancer,

00:34:01.230 --> 00:34:03.750
and what that does is it
gives you an IP address

00:34:03.750 --> 00:34:07.447
that your clients on the public
internet can send traffic to.

00:34:07.447 --> 00:34:09.239
And so that might look
something like this.

00:34:09.239 --> 00:34:12.750
Your client sends traffic to
your global load balancer.

00:34:12.750 --> 00:34:15.405
And lets say your client
happens to be in us-central1.

00:34:15.405 --> 00:34:17.280
Well, the global load
balancer's smart enough

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:19.590
to send that traffic
to us-central1.

00:34:19.590 --> 00:34:20.860
That minimizes latency.

00:34:20.860 --> 00:34:22.564
That minimizes cost.

00:34:22.564 --> 00:34:25.120
And so now your
retail front end,

00:34:25.120 --> 00:34:27.190
which is what they're
interacting with--

00:34:27.190 --> 00:34:28.940
they add something to
their shopping cart,

00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:30.482
and your retail
front end needs to be

00:34:30.482 --> 00:34:32.280
able to reach the shopping cart.

00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:34.350
That shopping cart might
be on Compute Engine.

00:34:34.350 --> 00:34:37.440
And it might just happen that
for some reason in us-central1,

00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:39.250
well, your shopping
cart is down.

00:34:39.250 --> 00:34:41.489
Maybe you deployed a new
version of your service

00:34:41.489 --> 00:34:42.540
that didn't work.

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:46.770
Maybe you've got some
planned downtime.

00:34:46.770 --> 00:34:48.690
And so what would
happen here is rather

00:34:48.690 --> 00:34:51.239
than having your retail
front end send traffic

00:34:51.239 --> 00:34:54.679
to the shopping cart and hit
errors for that end user,

00:34:54.679 --> 00:34:56.820
that traffic just goes
to your shopping cart

00:34:56.820 --> 00:34:58.110
in asia-southeast1.

00:34:58.110 --> 00:35:00.608
It just fails over
seamlessly to another region.

00:35:00.608 --> 00:35:02.400
And this is actually
pretty straightforward

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:03.720
to set up in Traffic Director.

00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:06.630
You just add back ends
in multiple regions

00:35:06.630 --> 00:35:09.393
as part of your service,
and Traffic Director

00:35:09.393 --> 00:35:10.935
will make sure that
your retail front

00:35:10.935 --> 00:35:13.820
end knows how to reach
the ones that are healthy.

00:35:13.820 --> 00:35:16.470
And so the shopping cart can
go to your payment service.

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:17.730
All's good.

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:19.980
Your customer gets
served despite the fact

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:22.050
that your shopping
cart in us-central1

00:35:22.050 --> 00:35:23.940
happened to be down.

00:35:23.940 --> 00:35:26.505
That automatic failover
for high availability,

00:35:26.505 --> 00:35:29.670
super critical in
the type of business

00:35:29.670 --> 00:35:33.300
where you have to make sure
that every request is handled

00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:36.030
in a high-quality way and gives
a good end-user experience

00:35:36.030 --> 00:35:37.650
because if that
doesn't happen, that's

00:35:37.650 --> 00:35:40.920
money left on the table
for you as a business.

00:35:40.920 --> 00:35:42.780
Another use case I see--

00:35:42.780 --> 00:35:45.390
I've got a customer who's one
of the world's leading logistics

00:35:45.390 --> 00:35:48.180
providers is that
they have to make sure

00:35:48.180 --> 00:35:50.400
that when they deploy a
new version of the service,

00:35:50.400 --> 00:35:52.375
they don't have any downtime.

00:35:52.375 --> 00:35:54.000
And so just for a
little bit of context

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:58.170
here, this customer has a
prediction service running,

00:35:58.170 --> 00:36:00.570
and what that service
does is it tries

00:36:00.570 --> 00:36:03.330
to figure out when a package
is going to be delivered.

00:36:03.330 --> 00:36:06.330
Well, they do that by training
a machine-learning model

00:36:06.330 --> 00:36:10.140
and deploying that model as
part of a workload that's

00:36:10.140 --> 00:36:11.850
running on Kubernetes Engine.

00:36:11.850 --> 00:36:14.130
They need to retrain that
model every once in a while

00:36:14.130 --> 00:36:15.620
and redeploy that.

00:36:15.620 --> 00:36:18.120
And so what that means is
when they do that deployment,

00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:20.730
they need to make sure that
that model actually works

00:36:20.730 --> 00:36:23.640
and that the deployment
happens successfully.

00:36:23.640 --> 00:36:26.220
Anyone who's done deployments
knows that a deployment

00:36:26.220 --> 00:36:27.930
is inherently risky.

00:36:27.930 --> 00:36:30.210
You might try to make sure
that you have a development

00:36:30.210 --> 00:36:32.370
environment, a staging
environment, a production

00:36:32.370 --> 00:36:35.130
environment, and sort of
qualify that new version as it

00:36:35.130 --> 00:36:38.960
goes through, but no two
environments are identical.

00:36:38.960 --> 00:36:41.695
And so even if do everything
in staging and validate

00:36:41.695 --> 00:36:43.070
that it works in
staging, there's

00:36:43.070 --> 00:36:45.800
still some risk that once
you get to production,

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:46.860
it's going to go down.

00:36:46.860 --> 00:36:49.790
And so this is a great example
of how we see customers

00:36:49.790 --> 00:36:51.650
using Traffic Director.

00:36:51.650 --> 00:36:54.290
In this example, the front
end is sending traffic

00:36:54.290 --> 00:36:56.110
to the prediction service.

00:36:56.110 --> 00:36:59.010
There's a Prediction v1 which
is the old version, Prediction

00:36:59.010 --> 00:37:02.340
v2 which is the new
version of the model.

00:37:02.340 --> 00:37:05.660
The customer has set
a traffic-split policy

00:37:05.660 --> 00:37:09.200
which causes the front end to
send a percentage of traffic

00:37:09.200 --> 00:37:11.840
to the old one, a percentage
of traffic to the new one.

00:37:11.840 --> 00:37:14.540
And as you validate
that the model is good,

00:37:14.540 --> 00:37:17.910
you can continue to increase
that percentage of traffic.

00:37:17.910 --> 00:37:20.450
And so suddenly your
deployments are a lot safer

00:37:20.450 --> 00:37:22.820
because you only subjected
a small percentage

00:37:22.820 --> 00:37:24.400
of your traffic to
the new version.

00:37:24.400 --> 00:37:25.650
You've checked that it worked.

00:37:25.650 --> 00:37:27.192
If it didn't actually
work, well, you

00:37:27.192 --> 00:37:29.630
can flip that back to
100% to the old version.

00:37:29.630 --> 00:37:32.780
This gives you a lot of
safety around your deployments

00:37:32.780 --> 00:37:35.420
It also unlocks all kinds
of really interesting DevOps

00:37:35.420 --> 00:37:37.370
patterns like
blue-green deployments,

00:37:37.370 --> 00:37:38.900
canary deployments.

00:37:38.900 --> 00:37:40.970
These are the types of
things that organizations

00:37:40.970 --> 00:37:42.345
are doing these
days, and you can

00:37:42.345 --> 00:37:45.365
use Traffic Director to deliver
those types of use cases.

00:37:48.130 --> 00:37:51.300
Another really interesting one
is this idea of network edge

00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:53.070
services for GCP.

00:37:53.070 --> 00:37:55.400
And also this is new for
non-GCP environments,

00:37:55.400 --> 00:37:57.150
and I'll talk a little
bit more about what

00:37:57.150 --> 00:38:00.300
I mean by network edge
services in both GCP

00:38:00.300 --> 00:38:01.360
and non-GCP environments.

00:38:01.360 --> 00:38:04.480
So I've got plenty of
customers-- large banks,

00:38:04.480 --> 00:38:08.070
for example, who have
services that run in GCP,

00:38:08.070 --> 00:38:11.115
and they also have services that
run in their own on-prem data

00:38:11.115 --> 00:38:12.090
center.

00:38:12.090 --> 00:38:14.055
And that might be
a temporary state

00:38:14.055 --> 00:38:15.930
as they're migrating
everything to the cloud,

00:38:15.930 --> 00:38:17.400
but it also might be
a permanent state.

00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:19.567
There are all kinds of
really valid and good reasons

00:38:19.567 --> 00:38:22.110
why you might want to have
some workloads in the cloud

00:38:22.110 --> 00:38:24.750
and some workloads in on prem.

00:38:24.750 --> 00:38:26.260
And this is great, right?

00:38:26.260 --> 00:38:28.300
It makes a lot of sense.

00:38:28.300 --> 00:38:30.870
One of the things that
customers come to GCP for

00:38:30.870 --> 00:38:33.660
is our global load
balancer, and this

00:38:33.660 --> 00:38:36.140
is similar-- this is the same
as what I mentioned earlier.

00:38:36.140 --> 00:38:38.580
And this global load
balancer is the thing

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:41.730
that delivers to you this
group of services that I

00:38:41.730 --> 00:38:43.600
call network edge services.

00:38:43.600 --> 00:38:44.900
And so what do I mean by that?

00:38:44.900 --> 00:38:47.317
I mean that when a client sends
traffic to the global load

00:38:47.317 --> 00:38:50.700
balancer, there's all kinds of
different policies and services

00:38:50.700 --> 00:38:52.380
that you can apply there.

00:38:52.380 --> 00:38:54.840
An example is Cloud Armor.

00:38:54.840 --> 00:38:57.420
You want to make sure that you
have DDoS protection in place

00:38:57.420 --> 00:38:59.640
so that your clients
on the public internet

00:38:59.640 --> 00:39:01.590
aren't overloading
your back ends,

00:39:01.590 --> 00:39:05.070
and so this checks to make
sure that the traffic that's

00:39:05.070 --> 00:39:06.630
coming in is safe.

00:39:06.630 --> 00:39:09.520
You might have Cloud CDN
for content distribution.

00:39:09.520 --> 00:39:12.420
So this helps you deliver
results more quickly

00:39:12.420 --> 00:39:13.270
to your end users.

00:39:13.270 --> 00:39:14.687
It also saves you
a bunch of costs

00:39:14.687 --> 00:39:17.400
by caching different
static assets that are

00:39:17.400 --> 00:39:19.483
delivered by the load balancer.

00:39:19.483 --> 00:39:21.150
And the load balancer
also has all kinds

00:39:21.150 --> 00:39:22.980
of really interesting
features built

00:39:22.980 --> 00:39:25.950
into it, things like
a global Anycast VIP

00:39:25.950 --> 00:39:28.770
so that you get a single IP
address that you can use around

00:39:28.770 --> 00:39:33.250
the world, and your clients can
just address that IP address.

00:39:33.250 --> 00:39:35.440
It'll go to the
closest instance,

00:39:35.440 --> 00:39:38.700
and that's how you minimize that
latency, minimize that cost.

00:39:38.700 --> 00:39:42.480
And I mentioned these things
because customers look at this

00:39:42.480 --> 00:39:43.350
and they go great.

00:39:43.350 --> 00:39:45.750
I can use all of this
with my cloud service.

00:39:45.750 --> 00:39:49.340
I can make sure that if I have
a cloud service, it's protected.

00:39:49.340 --> 00:39:51.740
I'm caching things
the right way.

00:39:51.740 --> 00:39:55.860
But I as a bank, for
example, don't really

00:39:55.860 --> 00:39:58.770
have that same global
footprint that GCP has.

00:39:58.770 --> 00:40:01.410
I don't have points of presence
all across the world where

00:40:01.410 --> 00:40:04.140
I can deliver a global
Anycast VIP where

00:40:04.140 --> 00:40:09.420
I can do different security
mechanisms that are technically

00:40:09.420 --> 00:40:12.310
or for cost reasons infeasible
for me to deliver myself.

00:40:12.310 --> 00:40:14.310
Well, wouldn't it be great
if you could actually

00:40:14.310 --> 00:40:17.860
have that for your
on-premises services as well?

00:40:17.860 --> 00:40:20.160
And so one of the things
that we see as very critical

00:40:20.160 --> 00:40:21.720
for Traffic Director
is the ability

00:40:21.720 --> 00:40:24.420
to support more than just GCP.

00:40:24.420 --> 00:40:26.490
And so today what
I'm showing you

00:40:26.490 --> 00:40:30.990
is the ability to actually
use those edge services

00:40:30.990 --> 00:40:32.457
with your on-prem services.

00:40:32.457 --> 00:40:33.790
And so what does that look like?

00:40:33.790 --> 00:40:38.640
Well, that looks like a pool
of proxies deployed here

00:40:38.640 --> 00:40:41.202
on a Compute Engine-managed
instance group.

00:40:41.202 --> 00:40:42.660
That managed instance
group is just

00:40:42.660 --> 00:40:43.950
part of our infrastructure.

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:46.470
It scales up and
down dynamically.

00:40:46.470 --> 00:40:49.400
Each of those is
running a proxy,

00:40:49.400 --> 00:40:51.900
and it acts as a middle
proxy load balancer.

00:40:51.900 --> 00:40:54.750
It connects to Traffic
Director to get configuration.

00:40:54.750 --> 00:40:56.640
An example of the
type of configuration

00:40:56.640 --> 00:41:00.660
would be, hey, there are
these on-premises endpoints,

00:41:00.660 --> 00:41:03.060
each of them belonging
to the on-prem service

00:41:03.060 --> 00:41:04.600
that you see on the
right-hand side.

00:41:04.600 --> 00:41:06.600
Traffic Director knows
about that because you've

00:41:06.600 --> 00:41:07.980
configured it that way.

00:41:07.980 --> 00:41:10.400
Traffic Director lets
the proxy know about it.

00:41:10.400 --> 00:41:12.810
And so now the next time
a client sends traffic

00:41:12.810 --> 00:41:15.090
to the global load
balancer, that traffic

00:41:15.090 --> 00:41:16.470
can go into the middle proxy.

00:41:16.470 --> 00:41:18.720
That proxy then
forwards the traffic

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:20.310
onto your on-prem service.

00:41:20.310 --> 00:41:21.810
And so this is a
really powerful use

00:41:21.810 --> 00:41:24.060
case where you can get things
like Cloud Armor, Cloud

00:41:24.060 --> 00:41:27.420
CDN, and global Anycast
VIP, these network

00:41:27.420 --> 00:41:30.810
edge services for both
GCP services and services

00:41:30.810 --> 00:41:33.320
running in non-GCP environments.

00:41:35.960 --> 00:41:40.030
We also see-- this is a
key investment for us which

00:41:40.030 --> 00:41:42.850
is this infrastructure
is really interesting,

00:41:42.850 --> 00:41:46.060
but how do you go
from zero to deployed?

00:41:46.060 --> 00:41:48.230
Well, we want to make
that as easy as possible,

00:41:48.230 --> 00:41:50.530
and so what you see
here is the current way

00:41:50.530 --> 00:41:53.710
of deploying something that
you would call a service mesh.

00:41:53.710 --> 00:41:55.810
You have some Compute
Engine services.

00:41:55.810 --> 00:41:57.787
These could be
Kubernetes as well.

00:41:57.787 --> 00:41:59.620
You've got maybe a front
end and a back end.

00:41:59.620 --> 00:42:01.870
Each of them has
a sidecar proxy.

00:42:01.870 --> 00:42:05.180
And setting this up,
it's not that hard,

00:42:05.180 --> 00:42:07.470
but it does require
some thought.

00:42:07.470 --> 00:42:09.520
Where do I get this proxy?

00:42:09.520 --> 00:42:11.213
What's the version
that I want to use?

00:42:11.213 --> 00:42:12.880
How do I make sure
the version is secure

00:42:12.880 --> 00:42:16.150
and I keep it up to date?

00:42:16.150 --> 00:42:19.090
You have to do things
like configure IP tables.

00:42:19.090 --> 00:42:22.125
And if you're like
me, before I started

00:42:22.125 --> 00:42:23.500
even looking at
Traffic Director,

00:42:23.500 --> 00:42:25.030
I had never even
messed with IP tables.

00:42:25.030 --> 00:42:26.655
And I talked to
customers who are like,

00:42:26.655 --> 00:42:30.080
this thing is really scary
because if you misconfigure it,

00:42:30.080 --> 00:42:32.320
well, you can suddenly drop
a whole bunch of traffic,

00:42:32.320 --> 00:42:34.040
and you don't want
to have to do that.

00:42:34.040 --> 00:42:36.310
And so a lot of customers
find that deploying

00:42:36.310 --> 00:42:38.640
sidecar proxies, not the
easiest thing in the world.

00:42:38.640 --> 00:42:40.880
So we want to make that
a lot easier for you.

00:42:40.880 --> 00:42:43.150
And so another thing that
I'm showing here today

00:42:43.150 --> 00:42:46.470
is a new way of deploying
your sidecar proxies.

00:42:46.470 --> 00:42:49.360
And a really easy way to
think about it is it's

00:42:49.360 --> 00:42:51.593
basically a virtual
machine template.

00:42:51.593 --> 00:42:53.260
It's a virtual machine
template that you

00:42:53.260 --> 00:42:55.840
control with some flags,
with some parameters--

00:42:55.840 --> 00:42:58.270
--service-proxy enabled.

00:42:58.270 --> 00:43:00.460
And what that will do
is it will automatically

00:43:00.460 --> 00:43:02.530
onto your virtual
machines, which

00:43:02.530 --> 00:43:04.960
could be running a client
application or a server

00:43:04.960 --> 00:43:06.160
application too--

00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:08.080
onto those virtual
machines, the template

00:43:08.080 --> 00:43:11.470
will install Envoy, a recent
version that we've qualified.

00:43:11.470 --> 00:43:13.690
It will connect it
to Traffic Director.

00:43:13.690 --> 00:43:15.990
It will do things like
set up IP tables for you

00:43:15.990 --> 00:43:18.645
so you don't have to deal
with things that might be very

00:43:18.645 --> 00:43:20.020
foreign to you
which, again, have

00:43:20.020 --> 00:43:23.410
very little to do with actually
delivering your services.

00:43:23.410 --> 00:43:26.850
And it gives you a really
easy way to do updates.

00:43:26.850 --> 00:43:29.860
It's as easy as issuing
a couple of commands

00:43:29.860 --> 00:43:32.440
to initiate a rolling update.

00:43:32.440 --> 00:43:35.260
You don't need to think
about picking an Envoy,

00:43:35.260 --> 00:43:37.420
compiling that Envoy,
the Envoy proxy,

00:43:37.420 --> 00:43:39.880
compiling it, deploying
it, setting things up.

00:43:39.880 --> 00:43:42.620
That's all handled for you.

00:43:42.620 --> 00:43:45.910
And so another thing
that we're announcing--

00:43:45.910 --> 00:43:48.070
and Kelsey showed
this before-- is

00:43:48.070 --> 00:43:50.410
this idea of using an
application library instead

00:43:50.410 --> 00:43:52.240
of an Envoy proxy.

00:43:52.240 --> 00:43:54.160
We've got Traffic
Director support

00:43:54.160 --> 00:43:56.890
for proxyless gRPC services.

00:43:56.890 --> 00:43:58.580
And what do I mean by that?

00:43:58.580 --> 00:44:01.000
What I mean by that is
if you look at the most

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:04.210
recent versions of
gRPC, they support

00:44:04.210 --> 00:44:06.840
this protocol called xDS.

00:44:06.840 --> 00:44:09.850
XDS is a group of
APIs that are used

00:44:09.850 --> 00:44:13.540
by Traffic Director, the
control plane, and things

00:44:13.540 --> 00:44:16.540
like the Envoy proxy or
like gRPC to exchange

00:44:16.540 --> 00:44:19.940
information about the
world, about the network,

00:44:19.940 --> 00:44:22.210
about your topology, about
what the endpoints are

00:44:22.210 --> 00:44:23.890
that you want to reach.

00:44:23.890 --> 00:44:28.000
And so much the same way as
when you have Envoy deployed,

00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:31.150
when you're using a
recent version of gRPC,

00:44:31.150 --> 00:44:33.490
you can provide it some
configuration when you start it

00:44:33.490 --> 00:44:33.990
up.

00:44:33.990 --> 00:44:37.180
You use a different
xDS resolver.

00:44:37.180 --> 00:44:38.710
It connects to Traffic Director.

00:44:38.710 --> 00:44:41.020
Traffic Director
tells that proxy

00:44:41.020 --> 00:44:43.900
what are the different endpoints
associated with the services

00:44:43.900 --> 00:44:45.340
that you're trying to reach?

00:44:45.340 --> 00:44:48.580
Because you'll notice very
quickly if you've used gRPC

00:44:48.580 --> 00:44:51.400
before, it's a super-powerful
application framework,

00:44:51.400 --> 00:44:53.200
but it doesn't come
with everything

00:44:53.200 --> 00:44:56.020
that you need to do a
distributed service.

00:44:56.020 --> 00:44:58.000
You see a lot of customers
who are doing things

00:44:58.000 --> 00:44:59.920
like adding an Envoy proxy.

00:44:59.920 --> 00:45:02.590
So your application
goes to that Envoy proxy

00:45:02.590 --> 00:45:04.960
which then sends the traffic,
and that's totally fine.

00:45:04.960 --> 00:45:06.130
It's super feature rich.

00:45:06.130 --> 00:45:08.420
It works really well
for a lot of customers.

00:45:08.420 --> 00:45:11.140
But if you're finding
that adding an Envoy proxy

00:45:11.140 --> 00:45:15.070
is something that maybe you
can't do for reasons like you

00:45:15.070 --> 00:45:18.280
can't really add a
sidecar, you can't

00:45:18.280 --> 00:45:21.220
mess with IP tables,
those types of things,

00:45:21.220 --> 00:45:24.130
well, now you have another way
where if you're using gRPC,

00:45:24.130 --> 00:45:26.830
you can very easily get the
benefits of service mesh

00:45:26.830 --> 00:45:28.600
simply by updating
to a new version,

00:45:28.600 --> 00:45:31.780
changing your resolver
to the xDS resolver,

00:45:31.780 --> 00:45:34.343
and connecting it
to Traffic Director.

00:45:34.343 --> 00:45:36.760
And this gives you already
straight out of the box service

00:45:36.760 --> 00:45:37.510
discovery.

00:45:37.510 --> 00:45:40.153
What are the endpoints
that I'm trying to reach?

00:45:40.153 --> 00:45:41.320
It gives you load balancing.

00:45:41.320 --> 00:45:43.737
And we're working on all kinds
of other interesting things

00:45:43.737 --> 00:45:46.660
that are super relevant here,
things like more traffic

00:45:46.660 --> 00:45:48.700
management, and so
stay tuned for that.

00:45:48.700 --> 00:45:50.200
You're going to see
that the goal is

00:45:50.200 --> 00:45:52.900
that we bring all of
the capabilities that

00:45:52.900 --> 00:45:56.290
are relevant to gRPC that you
can get in something like Envoy

00:45:56.290 --> 00:45:58.030
to gRPC as well.

00:45:58.030 --> 00:46:01.420
So example benefits
here are much easier way

00:46:01.420 --> 00:46:02.860
to adopt service mesh.

00:46:02.860 --> 00:46:04.233
Also performance.

00:46:04.233 --> 00:46:05.650
We found that, in
some cases, this

00:46:05.650 --> 00:46:08.650
can be more performant than if
you have to go through an Envoy

00:46:08.650 --> 00:46:09.370
proxy first.

00:46:09.370 --> 00:46:11.870
And obviously this is going to
depend on your use case, what

00:46:11.870 --> 00:46:14.278
you're doing exactly, but
also a very interesting thing

00:46:14.278 --> 00:46:16.570
to explore, especially if
you're already thinking about

00:46:16.570 --> 00:46:18.460
gRPC for performance benefits.

00:46:18.460 --> 00:46:21.310
This can get you even further.

00:46:21.310 --> 00:46:23.150
And so with that, I
want to wrap it up

00:46:23.150 --> 00:46:25.715
and say thank you very
much for listening today.

00:46:25.715 --> 00:46:27.590
If you've got questions,
there's a Dory where

00:46:27.590 --> 00:46:28.670
you can ask those questions.

00:46:28.670 --> 00:46:30.090
We'll be looking
at the questions,

00:46:30.090 --> 00:46:31.760
trying to get them
answered for you.

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:34.010
If you're new to Traffic
Director, check out the docs.

00:46:34.010 --> 00:46:36.010
There's a setup guide
that walks you through end

00:46:36.010 --> 00:46:37.970
to end how this stuff works.

00:46:37.970 --> 00:46:40.610
And also some of the things
that I showed you today

00:46:40.610 --> 00:46:43.470
may not be released yet at
the time where this comes out.

00:46:43.470 --> 00:46:46.310
So if you have questions,
drop them in the Dory,

00:46:46.310 --> 00:46:48.110
or I created this
form that you can use.

00:46:48.110 --> 00:46:48.860
Go to the form.

00:46:48.860 --> 00:46:50.030
Check it out.

00:46:50.030 --> 00:46:52.370
You can request access, and
we'll try to get back to you

00:46:52.370 --> 00:46:54.240
as soon as possible.

00:46:54.240 --> 00:46:56.120
So with that, I
want to say thank

00:46:56.120 --> 00:46:57.470
you very much for having me.

00:46:57.470 --> 00:46:58.970
Big shoutout to the
Traffic Director

00:46:58.970 --> 00:47:00.980
team who keeps pushing
